# ── The signing keyring ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# genesi.gpg is BINARY. Git's auto-detection would probably get this right (a
# keyring is full of NUL bytes), but "probably" is not a property you want on
# the file that decides what a machine will install as root: any newline
# translation corrupts it, and a corrupted keyring does not fail loudly — it
# verifies nothing and every signature check reports an unknown key.
genesi-arch/packages/genesi-keyring/genesi.gpg binary
# The trust and revocation lists are the opposite: pacman-key reads them LINE BY
# LINE and feeds each line to gpg as a key id. A CRLF checkout would hand gpg
# "<fingerprint>\r", which it rejects — so the key installs and is never
# trusted, which surfaces only on the first machine moved to SigLevel=Required.
genesi-arch/packages/genesi-keyring/genesi-trusted text eol=lf
genesi-arch/packages/genesi-keyring/genesi-revoked text eol=lf

